INT: Look for you.

EL: --for his children. So I know he-- and it was in that area, was I know where he was. He survived.

INT: Were you--

EL: He died here in the United States, this man. Was my-- friend of my-- my father. He was a little older than my father.

INT: Were you liberated from Georgenthal?

EL: Yes.

INT: By whom?

EL: The Russians. The Russians liberated us and almost killed us.

INT: Why is that?

EL: Well, they were, you know, on-- on the front for so many years in the military. And here, they-- they came across our whole-- we were at this-- this camp was a deserted factory, an emptied-out factory. It was, originally, I don't know, what kind of a factory. But that's what it was. And it was turned into a camp. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

And-- and when-- when they liberated us-- and we were, you know, all young girls, from 16 years, let's say, 30, 35, the oldest, if there were 30-- 30. I don't even know. But all young people, young girls.

